Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:05:18.11,BottomBar,,30,30,22,,👍 Like   •   💬 Comment   •   [▶ Subscribe]   •   ByteVis
Dialogue: 1,0:00:00.00,0:00:01.40,Default,,200,200,85,,How many times would you have to
Dialogue: 1,0:00:01.40,0:00:03.03,Default,,200,200,85,,fold a sheet of paper to reach
Dialogue: 1,0:00:03.03,0:00:05.33,Default,,200,200,85,,the Moon Take a guess Thousands
Dialogue: 1,0:00:05.44,0:00:07.63,Default,,200,200,85,,Millions The real answer is far
Dialogue: 1,0:00:07.63,0:00:09.96,Default,,200,200,85,,smaller and finding it takes one tool
Dialogue: 1,0:00:10.10,0:00:11.73,Default,,200,200,85,,a log By the end of this
Dialogue: 1,0:00:11.73,0:00:13.11,Default,,200,200,85,,video you'll work it out in your
Dialogue: 1,0:00:13.11,0:00:17.10,Default,,200,200,85,,head No calculator Here's the setup A
Dialogue: 1,0:00:17.10,0:00:18.63,Default,,200,200,85,,sheet of paper is about a tenth
Dialogue: 1,0:00:18.63,0:00:20.74,Default,,200,200,85,,of a millimeter thick Fold it once
Dialogue: 1,0:00:20.78,0:00:22.84,Default,,200,200,85,,and you have two layers Fold again
Dialogue: 1,0:00:22.90,0:00:25.35,Default,,200,200,85,,four Then eight sixteen thirty-two
Dialogue: 1,0:00:25.70,0:00:28.35,Default,,200,200,85,,Every fold doubles the thickness After
Dialogue: 1,0:00:28.35,0:00:30.29,Default,,200,200,85,,any number of folds the stack is
Dialogue: 1,0:00:30.29,0:00:32.35,Default,,200,200,85,,two multiplied by itself that many
Dialogue: 1,0:00:32.35,0:00:35.33,Default,,200,200,85,,times Now flip the question Instead of
Dialogue: 1,0:00:35.33,0:00:37.60,Default,,200,200,85,,asking how thick the stack gets ask
Dialogue: 1,0:00:37.60,0:00:39.14,Default,,200,200,85,,how many doublings it takes to reach
Dialogue: 1,0:00:39.14,0:00:41.63,Default,,200,200,85,,a target That's a log A log
Dialogue: 1,0:00:41.63,0:00:43.98,Default,,200,200,85,,answers one question how many times do
Dialogue: 1,0:00:43.98,0:00:46.34,Default,,200,200,85,,I multiply Take eight How many twos
Dialogue: 1,0:00:46.34,0:00:48.29,Default,,200,200,85,,do I multiply together to make it
Dialogue: 1,0:00:48.43,0:00:51.19,Default,,200,200,85,,Two times two times two Three twos
Dialogue: 1,0:00:51.31,0:00:52.85,Default,,200,200,85,,So log base two of eight is
Dialogue: 1,0:00:52.85,0:00:56.07,Default,,200,200,85,,three Same idea with tens A thousand
Dialogue: 1,0:00:56.07,0:00:58.33,Default,,200,200,85,,is ten times ten times ten so
Dialogue: 1,0:00:58.33,0:01:00.03,Default,,200,200,85,,log base ten of a thousand is
Dialogue: 1,0:01:00.03,0:01:02.64,Default,,200,200,85,,three That's the whole definition A
Dialogue: 1,0:01:02.64,0:01:04.92,Default,,200,200,85,,log counts multiplications It's the
Dialogue: 1,0:01:04.92,0:01:07.69,Default,,200,200,85,,exponent Pause and try one What is
Dialogue: 1,0:01:07.69,0:01:11.64,Default,,200,200,85,,log base two of thirty-two Five Two
Dialogue: 1,0:01:11.64,0:01:13.48,Default,,200,200,85,,times two times two times two times
Dialogue: 1,0:01:13.48,0:01:16.22,Default,,200,200,85,,two is thirty-two So our paper problem
Dialogue: 1,0:01:16.22,0:01:18.23,Default,,200,200,85,,is a log How many doublings make
Dialogue: 1,0:01:18.23,0:01:20.17,Default,,200,200,85,,the stack reach the Moon Hold that
Dialogue: 1,0:01:20.17,0:01:23.13,Default,,200,200,85,,thought Let's draw it Folds along the
Dialogue: 1,0:01:23.13,0:01:25.61,Default,,200,200,85,,bottom layers up the side The curve
Dialogue: 1,0:01:25.61,0:01:27.91,Default,,200,200,85,,starts slowly then rockets up That's
Dialogue: 1,0:01:27.91,0:01:30.95,Default,,200,200,85,,exponential growth Now swap the axes
Dialogue: 1,0:01:31.08,0:01:32.98,Default,,200,200,85,,Layers along the bottom folds up the
Dialogue: 1,0:01:32.98,0:01:35.87,Default,,200,200,85,,side It's the same curve flipped This
Dialogue: 1,0:01:35.87,0:01:37.78,Default,,200,200,85,,is the graph of a log Watch
Dialogue: 1,0:01:37.78,0:01:39.71,Default,,200,200,85,,how it behaves It shoots up at
Dialogue: 1,0:01:39.71,0:01:42.14,Default,,200,200,85,,first then flattens And every time the
Dialogue: 1,0:01:42.14,0:01:43.93,Default,,200,200,85,,number doubles the log goes up by
Dialogue: 1,0:01:43.93,0:01:46.51,Default,,200,200,85,,exactly one One to two one step
Dialogue: 1,0:01:46.63,0:01:48.39,Default,,200,200,85,,Two to four one step Four to
Dialogue: 1,0:01:48.39,0:01:50.61,Default,,200,200,85,,eight one step Eight to sixteen one
Dialogue: 1,0:01:50.61,0:01:52.93,Default,,200,200,85,,more It never stops climbing but it
Dialogue: 1,0:01:52.93,0:01:55.73,Default,,200,200,85,,climbs incredibly slowly To get from
Dialogue: 1,0:01:55.73,0:01:57.66,Default,,200,200,85,,one to a million the log only
Dialogue: 1,0:01:57.66,0:01:59.99,Default,,200,200,85,,reaches about twenty The two curves
Dialogue: 1,0:01:59.99,0:02:02.12,Default,,200,200,85,,are mirror images across a diagonal
Dialogue: 1,0:02:02.12,0:02:04.69,Default,,200,200,85,,line A log is the undo button
Dialogue: 1,0:02:04.69,0:02:07.48,Default,,200,200,85,,for an exponent Now here's something
Dialogue: 1,0:02:07.48,0:02:09.87,Default,,200,200,85,,strange Take any big list of real
Dialogue: 1,0:02:09.87,0:02:12.75,Default,,200,200,85,,numbers city populations river lengths
Dialogue: 1,0:02:12.80,0:02:15.12,Default,,200,200,85,,prices Look at just the first digit
Dialogue: 1,0:02:15.12,0:02:17.18,Default,,200,200,85,,of each one You'd expect the digits
Dialogue: 1,0:02:17.18,0:02:18.79,Default,,200,200,85,,one through nine to show up about
Dialogue: 1,0:02:18.79,0:02:21.29,Default,,200,200,85,,equally roughly eleven percent each
Dialogue: 1,0:02:21.64,0:02:23.66,Default,,200,200,85,,But they don't The digit one leads
Dialogue: 1,0:02:23.66,0:02:25.79,Default,,200,200,85,,about thirty percent of the time The
Dialogue: 1,0:02:25.79,0:02:29.50,Default,,200,200,85,,digit nine Under five In 1881 an
Dialogue: 1,0:02:29.50,0:02:31.77,Default,,200,200,85,,astronomer named Simon Newcomb noticed
Dialogue: 1,0:02:31.77,0:02:34.10,Default,,200,200,85,,something odd in a library In the
Dialogue: 1,0:02:34.10,0:02:36.30,Default,,200,200,85,,books of logarithm tables the first
Dialogue: 1,0:02:36.30,0:02:38.07,Default,,200,200,85,,pages were far more worn than the
Dialogue: 1,0:02:38.07,0:02:40.02,Default,,200,200,85,,last He guessed that people were
Dialogue: 1,0:02:40.02,0:02:42.00,Default,,200,200,85,,looking up numbers starting with one
Dialogue: 1,0:02:42.00,0:02:44.24,Default,,200,200,85,,far more often And the reason is
Dialogue: 1,0:02:44.24,0:02:46.55,Default,,200,200,85,,hiding in a log Picture a log
Dialogue: 1,0:02:46.55,0:02:49.07,Default,,200,200,85,,ruler where equal distances mean equal
Dialogue: 1,0:02:49.07,0:02:51.41,Default,,200,200,85,,multiplying The stretch from one to
Dialogue: 1,0:02:51.41,0:02:53.41,Default,,200,200,85,,two takes up about thirty percent of
Dialogue: 1,0:02:53.41,0:02:55.55,Default,,200,200,85,,the ruler The stretch from nine to
Dialogue: 1,0:02:55.55,0:02:57.95,Default,,200,200,85,,ten Under five percent Think of
Dialogue: 1,0:02:57.95,0:02:59.38,Default,,200,200,85,,something growing by a steady
Dialogue: 1,0:02:59.38,0:03:01.64,Default,,200,200,85,,percentage each year Going from a
Dialogue: 1,0:03:01.64,0:03:03.65,Default,,200,200,85,,thousand to two thousand takes more
Dialogue: 1,0:03:03.65,0:03:05.88,Default,,200,200,85,,than six times as long as going
Dialogue: 1,0:03:05.88,0:03:08.59,Default,,200,200,85,,from nine thousand to ten thousand So
Dialogue: 1,0:03:08.59,0:03:10.13,Default,,200,200,85,,numbers spend more of their time
Dialogue: 1,0:03:10.13,0:03:12.44,Default,,200,200,85,,starting with a one Auditors use this
Dialogue: 1,0:03:12.44,0:03:14.87,Default,,200,200,85,,to spot fake data People inventing
Dialogue: 1,0:03:14.87,0:03:16.47,Default,,200,200,85,,numbers tend to spread their first
Dialogue: 1,0:03:16.47,0:03:18.87,Default,,200,200,85,,digits too evenly It only works on
Dialogue: 1,0:03:18.87,0:03:21.03,Default,,200,200,85,,numbers that span a wide range and
Dialogue: 1,0:03:21.03,0:03:23.48,Default,,200,200,85,,it can't prove fraud It just raises
Dialogue: 1,0:03:23.48,0:03:26.59,Default,,200,200,85,,a flag That log ruler shows up
Dialogue: 1,0:03:26.59,0:03:28.15,Default,,200,200,85,,wherever we need to squash a huge
Dialogue: 1,0:03:28.15,0:03:30.96,Default,,200,200,85,,range into small numbers Earthquakes
Dialogue: 1,0:03:31.10,0:03:33.35,Default,,200,200,85,,Each whole step up the magnitude scale
Dialogue: 1,0:03:33.35,0:03:35.30,Default,,200,200,85,,means ten times bigger waves on a
Dialogue: 1,0:03:35.30,0:03:37.63,Default,,200,200,85,,seismograph and about thirty-two times
Dialogue: 1,0:03:37.63,0:03:40.32,Default,,200,200,85,,more energy So a magnitude seven is
Dialogue: 1,0:03:40.32,0:03:42.22,Default,,200,200,85,,a hundred times bigger than a five
Dialogue: 1,0:03:42.57,0:03:45.14,Default,,200,200,85,,Acidity On the pH scale each step
Dialogue: 1,0:03:45.14,0:03:47.59,Default,,200,200,85,,down is ten times more acidic A
Dialogue: 1,0:03:47.59,0:03:49.67,Default,,200,200,85,,pH of three is a thousand times
Dialogue: 1,0:03:49.67,0:03:51.58,Default,,200,200,85,,more acidic than a pH of six
Dialogue: 1,0:03:51.93,0:03:54.61,Default,,200,200,85,,And sound Every ten decibels means ten
Dialogue: 1,0:03:54.61,0:03:57.41,Default,,200,200,85,,times more sound intensity A whisper
Dialogue: 1,0:03:57.41,0:03:59.84,Default,,200,200,85,,is about thirty decibels a jet engine
Dialogue: 1,0:03:59.84,0:04:02.11,Default,,200,200,85,,up close about one hundred forty
Dialogue: 1,0:04:02.28,0:04:03.47,Default,,200,200,85,,That's a difference of around a
Dialogue: 1,0:04:03.47,0:04:05.80,Default,,200,200,85,,hundred billion times squeezed into
Dialogue: 1,0:04:05.80,0:04:08.82,Default,,200,200,85,,two numbers Same trick every time A
Dialogue: 1,0:04:08.82,0:04:10.75,Default,,200,200,85,,step on the ruler isn't adding It's
Dialogue: 1,0:04:10.75,0:04:13.51,Default,,200,200,85,,multiplying Now let's pay off that
Dialogue: 1,0:04:13.51,0:04:15.58,Default,,200,200,85,,promise How many folds to reach the
Dialogue: 1,0:04:15.58,0:04:17.99,Default,,200,200,85,,Moon The Moon is about three hundred
Dialogue: 1,0:04:17.99,0:04:20.32,Default,,200,200,85,,eighty-four thousand kilometers away
Dialogue: 1,0:04:20.50,0:04:22.00,Default,,200,200,85,,Our paper is a tenth of a
Dialogue: 1,0:04:22.00,0:04:24.37,Default,,200,200,85,,millimeter thick So the stack has to
Dialogue: 1,0:04:24.37,0:04:26.25,Default,,200,200,85,,get about three point eight trillion
Dialogue: 1,0:04:26.25,0:04:28.68,Default,,200,200,85,,times thicker Here's the mental trick
Dialogue: 1,0:04:28.82,0:04:30.30,Default,,200,200,85,,Two to the power of ten is
Dialogue: 1,0:04:30.30,0:04:32.22,Default,,200,200,85,,one thousand twenty-four Call it a
Dialogue: 1,0:04:32.22,0:04:34.73,Default,,200,200,85,,thousand So ten doublings is about a
Dialogue: 1,0:04:34.73,0:04:37.32,Default,,200,200,85,,thousand Twenty is about a million
Dialogue: 1,0:04:37.47,0:04:39.63,Default,,200,200,85,,Thirty a billion Forty about a
Dialogue: 1,0:04:39.63,0:04:41.97,Default,,200,200,85,,trillion Forty doublings gets us about
Dialogue: 1,0:04:41.97,0:04:44.77,Default,,200,200,85,,a trillion still short Forty-one isn't
Dialogue: 1,0:04:44.77,0:04:47.21,Default,,200,200,85,,enough But forty-two is a little over
Dialogue: 1,0:04:47.21,0:04:49.03,Default,,200,200,85,,four trillion and that's enough
Dialogue: 1,0:04:49.42,0:04:51.74,Default,,200,200,85,,Forty-two folds The same answer a
Dialogue: 1,0:04:51.74,0:04:53.51,Default,,200,200,85,,calculator gives worked out in your
Dialogue: 1,0:04:53.51,0:04:56.05,Default,,200,200,85,,head Of course a normal sheet only
Dialogue: 1,0:04:56.05,0:04:58.01,Default,,200,200,85,,folds about seven or eight times by
Dialogue: 1,0:04:58.01,0:05:00.93,Default,,200,200,85,,hand The record is twelve set by
Dialogue: 1,0:05:00.93,0:05:02.61,Default,,200,200,85,,a high school student named Britney
Dialogue: 1,0:05:02.61,0:05:05.00,Default,,200,200,85,,Gallivan using over a kilometer of
Dialogue: 1,0:05:05.00,0:05:07.78,Default,,200,200,85,,toilet paper But the math doesn't care
Dialogue: 1,0:05:07.88,0:05:09.46,Default,,200,200,85,,and that's the power of a log
Dialogue: 1,0:05:09.83,0:05:11.51,Default,,200,200,85,,A log just counts how many times
Dialogue: 1,0:05:11.51,0:05:14.47,Default,,200,200,85,,you multiply Forty-two doublings and a
Dialogue: 1,0:05:14.47,0:05:16.11,Default,,200,200,85,,sheet of paper reaches the Moon
